  "Question": "If \\[ \\frac{y}{x-z}=\\frac{x+y}{z}=\\frac{x}{y} \\] for three positive numbers \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z,\\) all different, then \\(\\frac{x}{y}=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac35\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac23\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac53\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Compare each of the first two fractions with \\(x/y\\). All denominators are nonzero because the numbers are positive and \\(x\\ne z\\). Cross-multiplication gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^2&=x^2-xz,\\\\\nxy+y^2&=xz.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAdding these equations eliminates \\(xz\\), leaving\n\\[x^2-xy-2y^2=0.\\]\nFactoring now separates the possible ratios:\n\\[(x-2y)(x+y)=0.\\]\nSince \\(x+y>0\\), we must have \\(x=2y\\).\n\nThe second cross-multiplied equation then gives \\(z=3y/2\\). For every \\(y>0\\), these values satisfy \\(x>z>y>0\\), and substitution makes all three original ratios equal to \\(2\\). Hence the required ratio is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
